judge.reasoning:
Both patches make the same runtime change in `__asyncHydrate`. They capture `wasConnected = el.isConnected` up front, and `performHydrate` bails out when `!el.parentNode || (wasConnected && !el.isConnected)`. This fixes the root cause the issue identifies: `hydrate()` running on a detached node. It also keeps hydration working for containers that were never attached to the document. Correctness is therefore identical.

The patches differ in tests and craft.

Patch A's tests cover detaching the root element and detaching an ancestor. They call the mocked observer callback directly. This works only because the same async component was resolved during `renderToString`, so the strategy registers synchronously. That is a somewhat implicit dependency. Patch A also has no positive test showing that a connected node still hydrates, and no test for a container outside the document. Without those, a regression from an over-broad guard would go unnoticed.

Patch B's tests are much more thorough:
- a detached single root;
- a detached fragment root, which exercises the anchor/comment path;
- a detached ancestor where the node keeps its parent;
- a positive case where a connected node hydrates and its event handlers work;
- a custom strategy in an off-document container, both the skip case and the hydrate case, which verifies the `wasConnected` nuance;
- no strategy, where the node is detached before the loader resolves.

These tests would fail before the fix and also guard against false negatives. Patch B also adds comments explaining the non-obvious `wasConnected` logic, while Patch A's one-line guard is unexplained.

Neither patch adds scope beyond the issue. With identical fixes, Patch B wins clearly on test quality and clarity.

